DAY 3 NOTES:

        C++ FUNCTIONS & VECTORS

    1. return_type function(parameters) -> General form of a function declaration
        Exp:
            int add(int a, int b) {
                return a + b;
            }

    2. void                             -> Means 'no value'. Function doesn't return a result
        Exp:
            void greet() {
                std::cout << "Hello";
            }
        
            Can call greret(), but not store the result
            YES greet();
            NO  int x = greet();

    3. std::vector<int>                 -> a resizable array in CPP standard library
        Exp:
            std::vector<int> numbers; // creates vector that stores int vals
            std::vector<int> numbers = {10, 20, 30};
            std::vector<double>
            std::vector<char>

    4. .size()                             -> returns the number of elements currently in a vector
        exp:
            std::vector<int> numbers = {10, 20, 30}
            std::cout << numbers.size(); // 3

    5. .push_back()                        -> adds an element to the end of a vector
        Exp:
            std::vector<int> numbers;
            numbers.push_back(10);
            numbers.push_back(20);

    6. [i]                                  -> Access element @ index i
        Exp: std::vector<int> numbers = {10, 20, 30};
        std::cout << numbers[0]; // 10
        std::cout << numbers[1]; // 20
        std::cout << numbers[2]; // 30

-- C++ REFERENCES:
        void change(int x) vs void change(int& x).
        Passing by value (void change(int x)) means the function receives a copy of the original value
        Passing by reference (void change(int& x)) means the function receives an alternative name or alias for the original variable
